{"type":"thinking","text":"Interesting: original-receipt.json's `sha` = 341e19e... which is the commit before HEAD. `sheetBlob` = 909e416... which matches shift-plan.json blob at HEAD (and at 341e19e presumably same). So the receipt is a \"vehicle bench receipt\" for the original sheet at commit 341e19e, with actual limits [80,40,50,70]. Its `withinLimits` false because requested [80,40,70,50] exceeds slot2 limit 50.\n\nThe task prompt (HEAD) asks to issue the current dispatch work order. The older TASK at 341 asked to check current sheet against available records, revise if necessary, etc. The current one asks for executable four-slot schedule, per-vehicle release slips, handover notes, explain departure energy, margin, low-efficiency handling.\n\nWe need to decide the correct revised schedule and release decisions. Key ambiguity: Should we trust the later DUTY-2340 receipt's limits, or the 21:00 cold-cycle? The release-format explicitly points to original-receipt.json as the archive of the original sheet's vehicle bench receipt; \"工况与适用范围见单内\" (conditions and applicability in the sheet). Its applicability says the station AC upper limits are fixed by time slot, and if the four half-hour slots, two-car windows, and 20 kW shift are unchanged, the limits can apply to the new setting. So yes: use limits [80,40,50,70]. That means the current plan [80,40,70,50] is not executable; must revise.\n\nWhat is the best executable schedule? \"排班保持现有字段、两车时窗、20 kW 已预约平移和高效工况下最大可用充电量。\" We keep fields, windows, 20 kW shift, and maximum usable charging amount under high efficiency. Need to produce a four-slot AC kW schedule that respects per-slot limits and maximizes available energy? Under actual limits and 20 kW shift, the max usable by buses is 110 AC. But A's window needs 60 AC exactly; B max 50 AC. The best is to charge A 80 in slot0 and 40 in slot1 (=60), B 50 in slot2 and 50 in slot3 (=50). That is [80,40,50,50]. Then A departure 126 (margin 0), B departure 121 (margin -9). Alternatively could we shift power so B gets more and A less? A must be GO if possible. A needs exactly 60 AC; its max is exactly 60 (slot0 cap 80, slot1 cap 40). So [80,40] is fixed for A. B then [50,50] max. So schedule [80,40,50,50] is the only reasonable \"maximum usable\" one. It is within limits [80,40,50,70], yes. At slot3 requested 50 <= limit 70. So actual = requested. B departure 121, deficit 9. A GO, B HOLD at 90%.\n\nBut could we improve B by moving more than 20 kW to slot3? Wait, moving more to slot3 reduces slot3 cap, making B worse, not better. Moving less (s<20) increases slot3 cap (up to 70) and decreases slot1 cap; A would lose. But could A compensate by charging more in slot0? A's slot0 cap is 80, already maxed. A needs 60 AC total; at s=20 A exactly 60. If s<20, A max <60 -> A HOLD. Could we increase A's slot0 above 80? Charger cap is 80, no. So s=20 is required to make A GO. Thus B stuck at 50 AC. So B HOLD at 90% is unavoidable given constraints. Unless we can change the shift amount (reserved 20). Even with s=0, A short 9; B GO. So either A or B must be short 9 at 90%. We prioritize A? The requirement \"逐车决定以车辆离场可用净电量为准\" and \"分车/低效率处置\" suggests decide per vehicle. Since A can be GO and B HOLD, we should keep A GO. Maybe we could allocate B's slot3 up to 70 by shifting the 20 kW into slot2 instead? Not allowed, shiftToSlot fixed 3. Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe the 20 kW shift is \"已预约\" and should be kept, but perhaps we can also adjust the schedule's slot 2 B charging to 50 and slot 3 to 70? No, slot3 building is 50+20=70, so cap = 120-70=50. Can't exceed 50 due to site limit (building+charging<=120). The station limit 70 doesn't bind; site limit binds. So B max is 50+50=100 kW*0.5=50 kWh AC.\n\nHold on: What if we charge B in slot 2 at 50 and slot 3 at 70 by shifting less than 20? At s=0, slot3 building=50, cap=70; B gets (50+70)*0.5=60 AC -> exactly 54 battery, GO. But A's slot1 building=100, cap=20; A gets (80+20)*0.5=50 AC -> 45 battery, departure 117, deficit 9. So one bus must be short by exactly 9. Which one? A's departure time 01:00, B's 02:00. Maybe priority to A? The existing handover says A is critical (9 kWh gap if no shift), B has margin. The 20 kW shift was designed to save A by sacrificing B's later capacity. It assumed B had enough. But with actual limits, B doesn't. Hmm.\n\nBut maybe there is another option: Use battery capacity headroom? B capacity 200, start 76, required 130, so headroom huge. A capacity 180, start 72. No issue.\n\nCould we make B GO by charging B more in slot2 (50) and slot3 (50) and accepting A HOLD? Then A departure 126? No, if s=20 A gets exactly 60 AC -> 126, GO. Wait, B is short. So only one can GO. Is there any way to have both GO by using more than 20 kW shift? Let's check s variable with actual station limits and A/B:\nA max AC = 0.5*(min(80, station0=80) + min(20+s, station1=40)) = 0.5*(80 + min(20+s,40)).\nFor s<=20: A = 50 + s/2. Needs 60 => s>=20. So s=20 exactly.\nB max AC = 0.5*(min(70,station2=50)=50 + min(70-s,station3=70)) = 0.5*(50 + min(70-s,70)).\nFor s=20, B=50. Need 60 => min(70-s,70)>=70 => s<=0. Contradiction. So no s satisfies both. Thus at 90%, impossible to have both GO under actual limits. We must produce an executable schedule and per-vehicle release decisions. Most sensible: prioritize A (the one that can be saved with the reserved 20 kW shift), B HOLD with -9 margin. Or prioritize B (s=0) and A HOLD. Which is expected by the puzzle?\n\nLet's examine clues. The original receipt says departureKwh [126,121], margin [0,-9]. It already shows the outcome under the old requested plan [80,40,70,50] clipped to [80,40,50,50], which is exactly [80,40,50,50]. So the receipt's actual departure corresponds to the best schedule we derived (A 126, B 121). So likely expected: revise shift-plan acKw to [80,40,50,50] (or keep [80,40,70,50]? No, \"可执行\" means setpoints within limits; depot_release_check checks each requested AC setpoint must fit its slot limit even if station clips actual power). So revise to [80,40,50,50]. Then release: at90 A GO, B HOLD; departureKwh [126,121]; marginKwh [0,-9]. At85: A? Need compute. At 85%, A's max battery = 60 AC*0.85=51 kWh, start 72 -> 123, required 126 -> deficit 3. B's max = 50 AC*0.85=42.5, start 76 -> 118.5, required 130 -> deficit 11.5. So at85 both HOLD. But maybe at85 we should re-optimize per-vehicle? At 85%, could shift less or more to balance; \"分车/低效率处置\" — per-vehicle/low-efficiency handling. The release format requires at85 decisions A/B. With current schedule [80,40,50,50] at 85%: A 123 (HOLD), B 118.5 (HOLD). But the old handover said at 85% best balanced leaves A 123, B 127 (departure) if we could shift differently (s=20 with old limits [80,40,70,50] giving A 123, B 127). Wait old model test says at 85%: normal A 123; cold A 114.5; cold B 130. Because model dispatch greedily allocates A first. With old limits, cold caps [80,20,70,50] -> A 50 AC*0.85=42.5 -> 114.5; B (70+50)*0.5=60 AC*0.85=51 -> 127? But test says B 130 because B gets 60 AC? 76+51=127, not 130. Hmm test line 110 says cold B 130 at 85%. Let me recheck dispatch behavior: dispatch processes bus A first, takes 25? Wait cold caps at 85% with no shift: building cold [40,100,50,50], caps [80,20,70,70? site 120-50=70, charger 80 => 70; slot3 70]. A needs 54/0.85=63.53 AC; available slots 0,1 = (80+20)*0.5=50, so A takes all 50 -> 42.5 battery -> 114.5, feasible false. B then starts with acEnergyLeft slot2=35, slot3=35; needs 63.53; takes both 35+35=70 AC -> 59.5 battery -> 135.5, but capped by battery capacity/required? dispatch amountToDeliverBattery = min(54, capacity-start=124)=54, acEnergy=63.53, so B takes 63.53 of 70, leaving 6.47; delivered battery 54 -> 130. Yes B 130. Fine.\n\nFor our actual limits, at 85%, if we schedule [80,40,50,50], A gets 60 AC -> 51 battery -> 123, B gets 50 AC -> 42.5 -> 118.5. Both HOLD. But perhaps at 85% we should rebalance to minimize worst deficit? Not required, but handov